**Evidence capped at 6 — the source audit is a false clean.** `blog-source-audit.mjs` reported 6 inline / 1 vague / 0 untagged, but it only inspects five named slots, and it returned "no quoted material in slot" for the empathy turn and the close *because the quotes there carry no attribution tag for it to read*. Outside the audited slots roughly 15 quotes run with no outlet, including the entire childhood section — the attic, the Listerine, the yearbook, the fur coat, "I don't think I had armor" — which is the origin of the thesis, the TL;DR's headline bullet, and the empathy turn's raw material. Worst instance: line 157 sources a named third party standing trial for a shooting death to "In 2024 he told it publicly," and the claim is re-asserted in the closer. All untagged load-bearing slots are named in the sidecar.

**Discoverability 6 — `faqs` is absent entirely**, along with `same_as`, `wikidata_qid`, `keywords`, and `citations`. Everything the writer controls is strong (60-char meta_title, head-term PASS, 149-char description, 13-word answer block, 6 of 8 search-intent H2s). Two of the four AEO bullets are simply at zero. The draft's own editor notes flag this. Gate recorded; non-binding at 7.5.

**What the draft does better than its recent neighbors:** it refuses the house empathy-turn grammar on purpose — puts the armor frame in his mouth and lets him reject it — and it has zero reader-command imperatives, against 2/3/4 in Penguinz0, KSI, and Brooke-Monk. Ledgers spot-checked against the body on three claims and all three held, including an honest disclosure that testimony quote #1 never made it into the text. The same-type similarity trip at 0.084 is a genuine false positive (colliding token is "New York Times" in an attribution fragment) — I re-ran it rather than taking the ledger's word.

